|term-group[ddd:domain][ddd:model][ddd:deep-model][ddd:refactoring-toward-deeper-insight]
  :status  proposed
  :source  "Eric Evans, Domain-Driven Design Reference: Definitions and Pattern Summaries (Domain Language, 2015), CC BY 4.0 — local copy ref/DDD_Reference_2015-03/"

  A **DOMAIN** is what a @{ddd:model} is *of*: a sphere of knowledge, influence or activity. A **MODEL** describes *selected* aspects of a @{ddd:domain}, so that problems in it can be solved. Because a model selects, one domain can have many models, and that is why Evans needs bounded contexts (@{ddd:bounded-context}). A **DEEP-MODEL** keeps the essential and drops the superficial, as judged by the people who know the domain. A model gets deeper through **REFACTORING-TOWARD-DEEPER-INSIGHT**: revision prompted by a new understanding of the domain rather than by technical convenience.

  |term[ddd:domain] A sphere of knowledge, influence, or activity. The subject area to which the user applies a program is the domain of the software.
    :source "Evans 2015, Definitions, p. vi — verbatim"
    :avoid  [domain] ; bare: MIT's AI Risk Repository uses "domain" for its seven top-level risk categories, a source term to translate
    |invariants
      - It is what a model describes, not the model: a model "describes selected aspects of a domain" (p. vi).
      - The definition carries two readings in its two sentences. The first is absolute: a domain is any sphere of knowledge, influence or activity. The second is relational: something is *the domain of* a program when a user applies the program to it. — our reading
    |collisions
      - MIT's risk "domains" (seven domains, twenty-four subdomains): categories in one source's taxonomy, not spheres of activity. Evans's own nearest term is *subdomain* ("Generic Subdomains", p. 41), a part of a domain factored into its own model.
      - "For a given domain of interaction" (Gabriel, Keeling et al. 2024) and "problem areas" (Kierans et al.): contexts of use for an alignment judgment, closer to a scope than to a domain in Evans's sense. — our reading

  |term[ddd:model] A system of abstractions that describes selected aspects of a domain and can be used to solve problems related to that domain.
    :source "Evans 2015, Definitions, p. vi — verbatim"
    :avoid  [model] ; bare: in this project's corpus "model" almost always means an AI model
    |rels :of @{ddd:domain} {1,1}
    |invariants
      - It is selective: it describes "selected aspects of a domain" (p. vi). Leaving things out is part of what a model is, not a defect.
      - It is defined by use, not by completeness: it "can be used to solve problems related to that domain" (p. vi).
      - It is a *system* of abstractions: the abstractions are related to one another. A list of terms is not yet a model. — our reading of "system"
      - Its language and the model change together: "Recognize that a change in the language is a change to the model." (p. 3)
      - "A model is a distillation of knowledge." (p. 39)
      - Many models of one domain are normal: "Multiple models are in play on any large project." (p. 2)
    |collisions
      - **AI model** (the trained network): the usual sense in the corpus, and the reason this lexicon doesn't use the bare word.
      - **This project's own "Model" row** in `CLAUDE.md`'s five-way table, "an ontology plus claims about how things behave". It differs from Evans's in both directions: it requires claims about behaviour, which Evans does not, and it drops the use ("can be used to solve problems"), which Evans requires. The two should be reconciled or the CLAUDE.md row renamed. — our reading
      - **Source model** (`influx/source-models/`): Evans's sense, applied to one source.
      - "Model alpha", "model beta", "model gamma": this project's iterations. Also Evans's sense.

  |term[ddd:deep-model] A model that sloughs off the superficial and captures the essential.
    :source "[SOURCE: Evans 2015, Refactoring Toward Deeper Insight, p. 8, modified — 'A model that sloughs off the superficial and captures the essential is a deep model.' restated as a definition]"
    |rels :is-a @{ddd:model} {1,1}
    |invariants
      - Depth is judged by the domain's own concerns: a deep model "captures subtle concerns of the domain experts" (p. 8).
      - It is reached, not designed in advance: "Sophisticated domain models seldom turn out useful except when developed through an iterative process of refactoring" (p. 8). — our reading; Evans says this of sophisticated domain models, and it is applied here to deep ones

  |term[ddd:refactoring-toward-deeper-insight] Refactoring of a model, or of its expression, motivated by an insight into the domain.
    :source "[SOURCE: Evans 2015, p. 8, modified — 'Refactoring can also be motivated by an insight into the domain and a corresponding refinement of the model or its expression in code.' restated as a definition; 'in code' widened to 'its expression']"
    |invariants
      - Its motive is domain insight, as distinct from refactoring "described in terms of code transformations with technical motivations" (p. 8).
    |practices
      - Iterate, with the people who know the domain: "an iterative process of refactoring, including close involvement of the domain experts with developers" (p. 8).

  |discussion
    - **What this project's domain is.** `CLAUDE.md` describes two things the project models: the frontier-AI risk landscape, and what the field's documents claim about it. Both are spheres of knowledge or activity in Evans's sense. Whether that is one domain, or two (the risk landscape, and the discourse about it) is open. The plan's two planes, "what was said" and "what it is said about", suggest two. — our reading
    - **The widening.** Evans's definitions tie a model to software ("the domain of the software"; the model's "expression in code"). This project's models are records and documents, not running software, so here "code" is read as whatever form the model takes. That widening is ours, and the entries it affects say so (@{ddd:refactoring-toward-deeper-insight}, @{ddd:ubiquitous-language}).
    - **A similar claim.** Joseph's refinement lesson (the value settles in the last 5–10% of many truthification passes) and Evans's "seldom turn out useful except when developed through an iterative process" say something similar, from different work. — our reading

  |examples
    |        Thing                                       |  Term here                                              |
    |----------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|
    | The frontier-AI risk landscape                     | a @{ddd:domain}                                         |
    | IASR 2026's account of that landscape              | a @{ddd:model} (implicit; a source's model, in its own context) |
    | The lexicon plus records this project is building  | a @{ddd:model} (ours)                                   |
    | Model beta's term files                            | a @{ddd:model}; conformist to IASR 2026 for its first risk definitions (plan §1.2) |
    | G4's pilot findings feeding back into the lexicon  | @{ddd:refactoring-toward-deeper-insight}               |

  |working-notes
    - Evans's "Definitions" section (p. vi) defines five terms: domain, model, ubiquitous language, context, bounded context. The context-mapping section (p. 28) adds three more: upstream-downstream, mutually dependent, free. Every other `ddd:` term restates a passage of Evans's as a definition, and its `:source` says which.
    - Open: whether "subdomain" and "core domain" earn entries. They matter if this project's risk analysis becomes a separate bounded context built on the lexicon (plan §3.3), which is close to Evans's core and generic subdomains.
    - Open: whether to reconcile `CLAUDE.md`'s "Model" row with this entry or rename the row (see the collision above).
